Imperial Decrees, Patents of Appointment, and Memorials [candidates are examined on one]:
Imitation of the Han-dynasty Imperial Decree Summoning Men of Worth and Virtue and Literature for Personal Policy Questioning [first year of the Yuanguang era (134 BCE)].
Imitation of the Tang-dynasty Patent of Appointment Making Wei Zheng a Specially Advanced Official [tenth year of the Zhenguan era (636)].
Imitation of a Memorial of Thanks for the Bestowal of Gilt-Silver Engraved Seals upon the Grand Secretaries Jian Yi, Yang Shiqi, and Others [second year of the Xuande era (1427)].

Legal Judgments [five items]:
Hereditary Succession and Protection Privilege of Officials;
Violating Deadlines in Receiving Tax Grain;
Prohibiting Official Welcoming and Sending Off;
Reporting Military Affairs;
Inciting Litigation.

Third Session

Policy Questions [five items]

Question: The learning of emperors and kings differs from that of commoners in plain cloth; it lies in attending to what is great. In benevolence, nothing is greater than loving one's亲 (kin); in righteousness, nothing is greater than honoring the worthy. Moreover, examining ancient teachings and roaming in the literary arts serve to broaden one's grasp of moral principles, mutually nourishing one another to achieve greatness. That whereby the sage-kings of high antiquity established the foundation of governance…
